\begin{abstract}
Multimodal emotion recognition reports margins without reporting how much room those
margins occupy. We measure two elementary quantities that set the scale of every such
margin. The
\emph{fusion headroom} $H$ is the gain of a paper's multimodal system over its own best
single modality, read from one table under one architecture; the \emph{majority-class
floor} is the score of a classifier that ignores its input. Reading eight matched
comparisons from five corpora at their source tables and measuring two more ourselves on
CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI, we find $H$ between $-1.8$ and $+9.1$ points: only the two EEG
corpora exceed $3.22$, the conversational, sentiment and peripheral-physiology benchmarks
stay at or below it, and four of the ten comparisons are negative. Re-measuring MELD with frozen encoders that never saw its
labels gives a headroom of $0.41$ weighted-F1 points against the published $2.81$,
because the text-only baseline rises by almost five. Mismatched architectures between
unimodal and fused rows multiply $H$ by $2.7$ on SEED-IV, and on MELD a constant classifier
already reaches $31.3$ weighted F1. We recommend reporting the floor, the headroom and a
chance-corrected metric with every margin.
\keywords{Multimodal fusion \and Emotion recognition \and Evaluation \and
Chance correction \and Missing modalities}
\end{abstract}

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}

Multimodal emotion recognition rests on the premise that text, speech, vision and
physiology carry complementary evidence, so that a system which fuses them should
outperform one that reads a single channel~\cite{poria2019meld,zhao2021}. Progress is
accordingly reported as a margin: a new fusion mechanism, or a new way of coping with
missing channels~\cite{lian2023,zhang2024,li2024}, is judged by how many points it gains
over the previous best. These margins decide which architectures the field adopts and
which robustness claims it accepts.

A margin, however, can be read only against the scale on which it is measured, and two
quantities that set that scale are rarely reported. The \emph{floor}: MELD's test split is
$48.1\%$ neutral, so a classifier that always answers \emph{neutral} scores $31.3$
weighted F1, and the single-modality and severe-missingness conditions that robustness
papers emphasise report scores a few points above it. The \emph{headroom}: if a paper's
best single modality scores $s^\star$ and its multimodal system $f$, then $H=f-s^\star$
is everything fusion is worth in that paper's hands, and a margin $\Delta$ over a
competitor consumes $\Delta/H$ of it. When a system reaches $60.25$ weighted F1 against a
previous $59.25$ on a corpus where fusion is worth $3.22$ points in total, the improvement
is not one point out of the forty left to a perfect score but one point out of three. We therefore ask a question that
the tables the field already prints can answer: how large is the headroom on the corpora
that multimodal emotion recognition uses, and how close to the floor do reported systems
operate?

The answer matters beyond bookkeeping. If fusion is worth only a few points, much of the
evidence for new fusion mechanisms consists of differences that are small against the
room available, and a metric that rises when a system collapses onto the majority class
can produce such differences by itself. Knowing the scale costs a subtraction and a closed
form; not knowing it directs effort toward architectures whose benefit the measurement
cannot resolve.

In this paper, we measure both quantities at their sources. Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a reading discipline for $H$ --- same paper, table, protocol and architecture ---
  and a demonstration that violating the last condition multiplies $H$ by $2.7$ on SEED-IV
  (Section~\ref{sec:diag});
\item an audit of ten matched comparisons over seven corpora, eight read at their source
  tables and two measured first-hand on CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI
  (Sections~\ref{sec:audit} and~\ref{sec:firsthand});
\item a first-hand re-measurement of a published MELD row with frozen modern encoders,
  showing that part of its headroom was room left by a weak unimodal baseline
  (Section~\ref{sec:meld});
\item closed-form floors and a reporting rule built on chance-corrected metrics, whose
  consequence we show on a missing-modality system of our own
  (Sections~\ref{sec:diag} and~\ref{sec:case}).
\end{enumerate}
We do not re-run published systems or claim that any published number is incorrect. $H$
as measured here is a property of the systems a paper compares, not an
information-theoretic bound on the corpus; Section~\ref{sec:limits} returns to this.

