\section{Related Work}

\textbf{Chance correction.} Powers~\cite{powers2011} established that recall, precision,
F-measure and accuracy are biased, that a system worse in informedness can score higher
under any of them, and that chance levels must be reported. Chicco and
Jurman~\cite{chicco2020} argue for the Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC) over F1,
while Delgado and Tibau~\cite{delgado2019} argue against Cohen's
kappa~\cite{cohen1960} in favour of MCC~\cite{matthews1975}, and a multidisciplinary
consortium has since catalogued such metric pitfalls in a domain-agnostic
taxonomy~\cite{reinke2024}. We claim none of this as
new. What we add is the measurement: no missing-modality emotion recognition paper we
examined reports a chance level for any degradation condition.

\textbf{Missing-modality emotion and sentiment recognition} divides into reconstruction of
the absent channel~\cite{zhao2021,lian2023} and reweighting that leaves it absent and
adjusts the fusion~\cite{zhang2024}; distillation from a complete-modality teacher spans
both~\cite{li2024}. These works report margins under severe missingness but not the
headroom those margins occupy.

\textbf{Fusion in conversational emotion recognition} has grown progressively richer:
from recurrent context over concatenated utterance features~\cite{poria2017}, to tracking
the state of each speaker~\cite{majumder2019}, to graph convolution over the speakers of a
dialogue~\cite{ghosal2019} and across modalities~\cite{hu2021mmgcn}; a recent survey catalogues these fusion families and
the evaluation strategies used with them~\cite{wu2025merc}. This line evaluates a
new mechanism by its margin over earlier systems, and the unimodal ablations some of these
papers report are not used to scale those margins. We use exactly such ablations to supply
the scale.

\textbf{Evaluation audits in NLP} have shown that standard splits change system
rankings~\cite{gorman2019} and that much of the field is statistically
underpowered~\cite{card2020}. This paper belongs to that genus: it measures how results
are reported rather than proposing a method.

