\section{Two Diagnostics}\label{sec:diag}

\subsection{Fusion Headroom and the Reading Discipline}

For a paper reporting a multimodal system and its unimodal ablations,
$H = f - \max_m s_m$, where $f$ is the score of the full multimodal system and $s_m$ that
of modality $m$ alone, all read from the same table under the same architecture and
protocol. For $H>0$, a margin $\Delta$ between two systems on that corpus has
\emph{utilisation} $\Delta/H$.

Three requirements govern the reading. \emph{Same paper and table}: combining a unimodal
score from one paper with a fused score from another compares training regimes, not
modalities. \emph{Same protocol}: splits, label sets and metrics must match.
\emph{Same architecture}: papers often report unimodal baselines with a shallow
classifier and the fused system with a deep one, so the difference contains both fusion
and architecture. Zheng et al.~\cite{zheng2019} report on SEED-IV, in one table, EEG alone
at $70.33\%$ and eye movements at $67.82\%$ (linear SVMs), feature-level fusion with SVMs
at $75.88\%$, and a bimodal deep autoencoder at $85.11\%$. Read against the deep model
$H=14.78$; read within the SVM family $H=5.55$. We report only matched values.

\subsection{The Majority-Class Floor}

Let a constant predictor emit class $k_0$, whose share of the \emph{evaluation} split is
$\pi_0$, among $K$ classes. Then accuracy, weighted F1 and macro F1 are, as fractions,
\begin{equation}
A = \pi_0,\qquad W = \frac{2\pi_0^2}{1+\pi_0},\qquad M = \frac{1}{K}\cdot\frac{2\pi_0}{1+\pi_0},
\end{equation}
since class $k_0$ has precision $\pi_0$ and recall $1$ and every other class has
$F_1=0$. Two assumptions are easy to lose: $\pi_0$ is the evaluation share of the class
\emph{actually predicted}, not the training majority, and macro F1 averages over the
label set, not over the classes observed in a split (a common library default). On MELD's
test split ($K=7$, $\pi_0=0.481$) the floor, in percent, is $W=31.3$, $M=9.3$ and
$A=48.1$.

\subsection{Which Metric Survives Collapse}

Let $T_\lambda$ emit a classifier's output with probability $\lambda$ and the majority
class otherwise. Since $T_a\circ T_b=T_{ab}$, lowering $\lambda$ provably destroys
information. Cohen's kappa is strictly increasing in $\lambda$ whenever it is positive,
and so are multiclass MCC and balanced accuracy along the same family; none can be raised
by collapse onto the prior. Weighted F1 has no such property: on 9{,}261 random confusion
matrices over MELD's prior with positive kappa, garbling to $\lambda=0.9$ raised kappa in
$0$ cases and weighted F1 in $9{,}041$ ($97.6\%$; Fig.~\ref{fig:garbling}). This is the
phenomenon Powers described~\cite{powers2011}, stated for the operation that severe
missingness approximates.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_garbling.pdf}
\caption{Collapse toward the prior ($T_{0.9}$) applied to 9{,}261 random confusion
matrices over MELD's test prior with positive kappa. Every matrix moves left (kappa
falls); 97.6\% move up (weighted F1 rises).}
\label{fig:garbling}
\end{figure}

The three chance-corrected metrics do differ in \emph{sensitivity} near the floor. Kappa
and MCC share the numerator $\lambda D$, with $D=p_{\mathrm{o}}-p_{\mathrm{e}}$ the gap
between observed and expected agreement, but MCC's denominator vanishes like $\sqrt{\lambda}$, so kappa moves
as $\Theta(\varepsilon)$ and MCC as $\Theta(\sqrt{\varepsilon})$ in the mass $\varepsilon$
predicted away from the constant class. Perturbing the constant predictor on MELD's test
prior by a single sample (one prediction flipped to \emph{fear}, the rarest class) moves MCC by $+0.024$ or $-0.009$ and kappa by $+0.0011$ or
$-0.0004$. Near the floor MCC is the more responsive instrument and kappa the more stable
one, which is a reason to report both --- the position Delgado and
Tibau~\cite{delgado2019} reach on other grounds --- rather than to choose between them.

