The range is $-1.8$ to $+9.1$ points (Fig.~\ref{fig:headroom}), and \textbf{four of the
ten comparisons are negative}: the fused system scores below its own best single
channel. The ten rows
are not ten independent experiments, since MELD, AMIGOS and KEMDy20 contribute two each.
The ordering also defies intuition. EEG with eye movements gives the largest headroom;
EEG with peripheral physiology, which shares the property that neither channel is
derivable from the other, is negative on both AMIGOS dimensions; and speech with wearable
biosignals is negative on arousal. The KEMDy20 authors explain their case in a way that generalises:
acoustic features already carry most of the arousal information, and electrodermal
activity and inter-beat interval mainly track arousal too. Complementarity is not the
absence of shared content; it is different content on the labelled dimension.

\textbf{Reading at the source.} We first assembled this table from search summaries, and
three entries were wrong, all in the same direction. KEMDy20 was recorded at $H=3.8$
because the paper's ``$+3.8$ points'' is measured against its speech-only baseline, not
the best single modality. MELD was recorded at about $6$ because summaries attributed the
text-only cell to the bimodal system. SEED-IV was recorded at $14.78$, the
cross-architecture reading above. Every correction moved $H$ downward.

\textbf{Distance to the floor.} The floor matters where it is close. On MELD's
severe-missingness conditions, single-modality systems report weighted F1 in the low
thirties against a floor of $31.3$. On AMIGOS, the best single modality reaches F1 $56.4$
against a random baseline of $50.0$ that the paper prints in its own table: $6.4$ points
of signal in total, inside which the fusion comparison of Table~\ref{tab:headroom} takes
place. A margin reported in such a regime measures, in part, which system collapses onto
the prior less completely.

