\section{First-Hand Measurements}\label{sec:firsthand}

\subsection{Protocol}

For three corpora we measured $H$ ourselves under one architecture: a masked
bidirectional LSTM~\cite{hochreiter1997} over the utterances of a video or dialogue, with
a per-utterance linear read-out and packed sequences, as in bc-LSTM~\cite{poria2017}.
We use hidden size $100$, dropout $0.5$, Adam with learning rate $10^{-3}$ and batches of
$32$, and train for at most $50$ epochs with early stopping (patience $8$) on a seeded
$20\%$ of the training videos, with five seeds ($0$--$4$). Only the input feature blocks change between
configurations. Fusion gets three chances: early concatenation, late averaging of the
unimodal posteriors, and per-modality encoders concatenated before the read-out (mid).
Both the fusion style and the best single modality are chosen on the held-out training
videos, never on test. As a robustness check, hyper-parameters are also selected per
configuration over hidden $\in\{50,100,200\}$ and dropout $\in\{0.3,0.5\}$. Differences
are paired over seeds and reported with 95\% confidence intervals and paired $t$-tests.

\textbf{Reproducibility.} The bi-LSTM runs on four CPU threads and is deterministic given
the seed: re-running MOSI in the same environment reproduced every seed of every
configuration exactly. The frozen encoders of Section~\ref{sec:meld} run in half
precision, inference only, on a single 6\,GB consumer GPU; extracting every MELD block
takes about an hour. Feature files are pinned by checksum, and each number in this paper
is recorded with the script that produced it.

