\section{What the Diagnostics Do to a Margin}\label{sec:case}

We built a missing-modality framework to the field's standard --- per-channel evidential
heads, a vacuity-weighted fusion gate and asymmetric distillation from a complete-modality
teacher --- on a controlled benchmark with MELD's class priors, ten seeds and seven
availability patterns. It reported $+5.06$ weighted F1 over the strongest baseline in the
visual-only condition. On that benchmark $H=7.3$, so the margin claimed $69\%$ of
everything fusion is worth; the system scored $36.0$ against a floor of $31.0$, and four
of the six systems compared scored below the floor. Under macro F1, which weights the
rare classes but is not chance-corrected, the margin shrank to $+0.66$. A five-seed re-run
that also logged chance-corrected metrics gives, in the same condition, $+4.82$ weighted F1
against $+2.47$ kappa points (we write kappa and MCC points for $100\times\Delta$). One
result survived all three checks in that re-run: averaged over the seven availability
patterns and against a matched-capacity attention gate, $+2.19$ weighted F1
($p=0.0095$), $+1.97$ kappa points ($p=0.011$) and $+1.84$ MCC points ($p=0.0077$). A
margin that is not an artifact of the metric looks like this: smaller, and consistent
across metrics that disagree about what to reward.

