\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\textbf{Why the headroom is small.} Three explanations fit Table~\ref{tab:headroom}, and
our data cannot yet separate them. The first is redundancy on the labelled dimension:
where channels carry overlapping information about the label, as acoustic and
physiological signals do about arousal in KEMDy20, the best channel already holds most of
what the others would add. The second is weak auxiliary channels: on MOSI, audio and video
alone score below a constant classifier, and on MELD a whole-frame visual encoder sits at
the floor, so fusion has little to extract from them; work on imbalanced multimodal
learning adds that multimodal and unimodal objectives can conflict during training, which
can leave a fused model short of what even its weaker channels hold~\cite{wei2024mmpareto}. The third is a dominant text
channel: in conversational and sentiment corpora the transcript carries most of the label,
and a stronger text encoder moves the unimodal baseline more than the fused system, which
is what the MELD re-measurement shows. The largest headroom belongs to SEED, where EEG and
eye movements are nearly equally informative on their own ($78.51$ and $77.80$), which is
consistent with the second and third explanations.

\textbf{What this means for published margins.} Utilisation makes the stakes concrete. A
one-point margin on MELD consumes more than a third of the headroom in the published bc-LSTM row; on
frozen features, where $H$ is $0.41$, the same margin would exceed everything fusion is
worth, and would have to come from somewhere other than fusion --- the text encoder, the
training schedule, or chance. We do not suggest that such margins are wrong. We suggest
that a margin should be reported beside the room it occupies, so that readers can tell a
fusion result from a representation result.

\textbf{Two ways a first-hand headroom inflates.} Even with matched architectures, our
measurements exposed two mechanisms that no results table reveals. Choosing the fusion
style by its test mean reported a MOSEI gain between $1.8$ and $5$ times larger than the
held-out data support, and model selection on a held-out split that the features have already seen
(MOSI's $99\%$ against $80\%$ on test) makes selection close to arbitrary. Both are cheap
to detect: report held-out beside test numbers, and choose on held-out data only.

\textbf{Relation to other audits.} Like the audits of standard splits~\cite{gorman2019}
and statistical power~\cite{card2020}, this is a result about reporting. Unlike them, the
quantity it adds can be computed from tables that papers already print, so an existing
literature can be re-read without re-running any system, which is what
Section~\ref{sec:audit} does.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limits}

\textbf{$H$ is a property of systems, not of corpora.} We measure the headroom realised
by the systems a paper compares, not a bound; a better fusion method could enlarge it. Two
things temper this. The corpora span eleven years and very different architectures, and
the range does not widen with capacity. And weak unimodal baselines bias $H$ upward, so
the small values are, if anything, upper estimates --- which Section~\ref{sec:meld} tests
on one row.

\textbf{Sample.} We read one paper per corpus, with two rows each for MELD, AMIGOS
and KEMDy20. The metrics differ across rows (accuracy, weighted F1, UAR and F1), so
values are comparable within a row, not across corpora. DEAP is missing because its tables are unreadable, which may not
be random.

\textbf{Released features.} Our MOSI and MOSEI rows use features whose text and video
extractors were trained on the training split: held-out training videos reach far higher
accuracy than test, which saturates model selection. The rows describe fusion over those
features. On the same release's IEMOCAP~\cite{busso2008} features, a model tested on
session 4 instead of session 5 gains $31$ points of accuracy from the released text block,
and replacing that block with any of three frozen encoders reverses the gap to about
$-7$, which suggests that such features can mislead under protocols they were not built for.
The MELD measurement avoids this, but covers a single corpus.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Recommendations and Conclusion}

For each ablation or degradation condition we recommend three numbers beside the metric
the field expects: the floor $2\pi_0^2/(1+\pi_0)$ from the label counts; the headroom $H$,
matched on paper, table, protocol and architecture, with the utilisation $\Delta/H$ of any
claimed margin; and a chance-corrected metric (kappa, MCC or balanced accuracy), reporting
kappa and MCC together near the floor. A margin that exists in weighted F1 and vanishes
under chance correction has not been shown to be a robustness gain, and a margin that
claims more than half the available headroom deserves suspicion first from its author.
The rule is in the spirit of consensus reporting checklists for machine-learning-based
science~\cite{kapoor2024reforms}.

What fusion is worth, on the corpora the field uses and in the hands of the systems it
compares, is small; a metric that rewards collapse onto the prior is the wrong instrument
for measuring small things; and part of the published headroom is room left by weak
unimodal baselines. Future work should repeat the first-hand measurements with frozen
modern encoders on MOSI and MOSEI, and ask which corpus designs would make fusion
valuable, which is a question about how emotion is expressed across channels rather than
about architectures.

